# Cache policy for the STATIC ASSETS, which src/server.ts never sees.
#
# Workers Assets serves these without entering the Worker's fetch handler, so
# lib/cache-policy.ts — which only reaches text/html documents — covers none of
# them. Until this file existed there was no policy at all and everything got
# Workers Assets' default of `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, including
# content-hashed bundles. That is wordle-teams-82zq, and it was measured wider
# than the issue described: /assets/index-*.js was revalidating on every
# request too, not just the OpenGraph image.
#
# THE APEX INHERITS WHATEVER THIS SAYS at cutover, so the values are chosen to
# be right for production rather than convenient for beta.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED, because immutable is the wrong answer for it:
#
#   /sw.js          THE ONE THAT MUST NEVER BE IMMUTABLE. The service worker is
#                   the file that controls every future update; a browser
#                   holding a year-old copy cannot be reached by shipping a new
#                   one. It keeps Workers Assets' revalidate-always default,
#                   which is correct.
#   /manifest.json  Production serves this immutable, but a manifest that can
#                   never change means the app name, theme colour and icons are
#                   frozen for a year in any browser that fetched it. Left on
#                   the default pending a decision — see the follow-up issue.
#   /robots.txt     A policy file. Short-lived is the point.
#   /favicon.ico    Production also serves this on the default, so leaving it is
#                   parity rather than an omission.
#   the /about screenshots  Stable names like the OpenGraph image, but far less
#                   load-bearing, and freezing eight PNGs for a year to save a
#                   revalidation on one page is not a trade worth making
#                   without asking.

# CONTENT-HASHED BY VITE, so the filename changes whenever the bytes do. This is
# the case immutable exists for and it carries no risk at all: a stale copy can
# never be requested, because a new build produces a new URL. Highest value here
# too — these are the JS and CSS every visitor fetches.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# EXACTLY WHAT PRODUCTION SENDS, byte for byte, which is the parity this issue
# asked for. Safe for the same reason lib/seo.ts already records: Facebook's and
# LinkedIn's scrapers cache the card image BY URL, so redrawing it means
# shipping it under a NEW FILENAME rather than overwriting this one. That
# constraint is pre-existing and is what makes a year of immutability free.
/opengraph-image.png
  Cache-Control: public, immutable, no-transform, max-age=31536000
